            <ArticleTitle>Analgesia after caesarean section. The use of rectal diclofenac as an adjunct to spinal morphine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>297-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A double-blind placebo-controlled study was performed to assess the analgesic effect of rectal sodium diclofenac 100 mg after Caesarean section using subarachnoid hyperbaric bupivacaine 0.5% and morphine 0.2 mg. During the 48 h follow-up period, both placebo and diclofenac groups had comparable analgesia as measured by visual analogue scores (VAS) at rest and on movement. However, diclofenac prolonged the mean time to first analgesia by more than 5 h from 13 h 45 min in the placebo group to 18 h 58 min (p &lt; 0.03). The incidence of side effects (nausea, vomiting, itching, excessive lochia loss and the need for additional analgesia) were comparable in each group.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
